---
import Layout, { REPO } from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { bookmarkletUrl } from "../../../packages/browser/bookmarklet-url.ts";
import bundle from "../../../packages/browser/bookmarklet.js?raw";
const href = bookmarkletUrl(bundle);
---

<Layout
  title="Install deadhead: CLI, Vite, ESLint and bookmarklet"
  description="Install and run deadhead: the CLI, Vite plugin, bookmarklet, ESLint plugin, suppression comments and CI configuration."
>
    <h1>Install</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Check HTML in your browser, from your terminal or as you develop.
      The CLI and ESLint plugin need Node 24.8 or newer and use ESM.
    </p>

    <h2>Try without installing</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/try">Paste HTML into Try</a> to see findings, explanations and
      the fixed HTML. To inspect a page you have open, use the{" "}
      <a href="#bookmarklet">bookmarklet</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>CLI</h2>
    <p>Check one file or a built site. No configuration is required:</p>
    <pre><code>npx deadhead index.html
npx deadhead dist</code></pre>
    <p>
      Run your project's build first, then replace <code>dist</code> with its
      HTML output directory. Astro and Vite use <code>dist</code> by default.
      Each finding gives you a file, line, severity and a link explaining
      what to change.
    </p>
    <p>To keep deadhead in your project:</p>
    <pre><code>npm install --save-dev deadhead</code></pre>
    <p>Add a script to your existing <code>package.json</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>&lbrace;
  "scripts": &lbrace;
    "lint:html": "deadhead dist"
  &rbrace;
&rbrace;</code></pre>
    <p>
      Run <code>npm run lint:html</code> after building, locally or in CI.
      Use <code>--fix</code> to remove fixable markup and report what remains.
      Apply changes to source templates too, so they survive the next build.
    </p>
    <h3>More commands</h3>
    <p>
      It takes files and directories, and expands globs itself so they
      behave the same in every shell:
    </p>
    <pre><code>npx deadhead "src/**/*.html"         # quote it; the CLI expands it
npx deadhead --format=sarif dist     # stylish (default), json, sarif
npx deadhead --fail-on=harmful dist  # exit 1 only on harmful findings
npx deadhead --fix dist              # rewrite files, then report what remains
npx deadhead --jobs 4 dist           # large trees in worker threads
npx deadhead --quiet dist            # only findings at or above --fail-on
npx deadhead --output-file r.sarif --format=sarif dist
npx deadhead --list-rules            # every rule, then exit
cat page.html | npx deadhead - --stdin-filename page.html</code></pre>
    <p>
      Pass <code>-</code> as the only input to check stdin.{" "}
      <code>--stdin-filename</code> requires <code>-</code>. Baselines and
      baseline updates require files, including baselines set in the config.{" "}
      <code>--jobs</code> must be a positive integer.
    </p>
    <p>
      Exit codes are the CI contract: <strong>0</strong> nothing at or above the{" "}
      <code>--fail-on</code> threshold, <strong>1</strong> threshold met,{" "}
      <strong>2</strong> usage, config or I/O error. A broken invocation never
      looks like a clean run.
    </p>

    <h2 id="vite">Vite</h2>
    <p>
      Add <code>deadhead/vite</code> to a Vite 8 project to see findings in the
      terminal and DevTools console as you open pages during development:
    </p>
    <pre><code>// vite.config.ts
import &lbrace; defineConfig &rbrace; from "vite";
import deadhead from "deadhead/vite";

export default defineConfig(&lbrace; plugins: [deadhead()] &rbrace;);</code></pre>
    <h3>Astro</h3>
    <p>
      In <code>astro.config.mjs</code>, put the plugin under{" "}
      <code>vite.plugins</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>import &lbrace; defineConfig &rbrace; from "astro/config";
import deadhead from "deadhead/vite";

export default defineConfig(&lbrace;
  vite: &lbrace; plugins: [deadhead()] &rbrace;,
&rbrace;);</code></pre>
    <p>
      Astro does not register Vite plugins from a top-level{" "}
      <code>plugins</code> property. Start the dev server and open a page to
      trigger a report. A clean page reports no findings; starting the server
      alone does not lint pages.
    </p>
    <p>
      Positions refer to the served HTML, after Vite's transforms. SSR routes
      served through Vite's middleware stack get the same check. The terminal
      prints again when findings change. Set <code>console: false</code> to keep
      output in the terminal and preserve response bytes and headers.
    </p>
    <p>
      The plugin reads <code>deadhead.config.ts</code> from Vite's root. Options
      override the file; rule settings merge. Globs match URL paths without a
      leading slash or query string. If the CLI config includes build files,
      override that for dev:
    </p>
    <pre><code>deadhead(&lbrace; include: ["**"], ignore: ["vendor/**"], console: false &rbrace;);</code></pre>
    <p>
      Checks cover completed UTF-8 HTML responses up to 2 MiB. Streamed chunks
      flow as they arrive, with a report after the response ends. Compressed and
      partial responses pass through. A CSP that blocks console output leaves
      the terminal report available; set Vite's <code>html.cspNonce</code> for
      pages that require a script nonce. Restart Vite after config changes.
      Keep <code>"postbuild": "deadhead dist"</code> for production and CI, after
      your framework finishes its build.
    </p>

    <h2>Pages and fragments</h2>
    <p>
      A file with a doctype or an <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> tag is a page.
      Anything else is a fragment: a layout partial, a component, a template.
      A fragment is checked for the markup it holds, never for head content it
      lacks, since other files supply that. Markup outside a written{" "}
      <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> gets both the head
      and the body rules.
    </p>

    <h2>Fixes are text edits, never re-serialised markup</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--fix</code> splices byte ranges out of the original file. It never
      parses your document and prints it back, because that rewrites quote
      style and attribute order across the
      whole file. Whitespace and character references shift with them, turning
      a one-line fix into a thousand-line diff.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two things are never fixed automatically: a rule declaring{" "}
      <code>fix: &lbrace; op: "none" &rbrace;</code>, and any rule whose
      detectability is{" "}
      <code>partial</code>. If the rule is not certain, it does not get to edit
      your file.
    </p>

    <h2>Suppressing a finding</h2>
    <pre><code>&lt;!-- deadhead-disable-next-line meta/http-equiv-x-ua-compatible --&gt;
&lt;!-- deadhead-disable link/shortcut-icon --&gt;
&lt;!-- deadhead-enable --&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      A bare <code>deadhead-disable</code> with no rule id suppresses everything
      until the matching enable. The engine reads the contents of{" "}
      <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;code&gt;</code>,{" "}
      <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;samp&gt;</code> and{" "}
      <code>&lt;kbd&gt;</code> as content rather than markup, and skips{" "}
      <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;noembed&gt;</code>,{" "}
      <code>&lt;noframes&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;noscript&gt;</code>,{" "}
      <code>&lt;plaintext&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> and{" "}
      <code>&lt;xmp&gt;</code>, which an HTML parser reads as text.
    </p>

    <h2 id="bookmarklet">Bookmarklet</h2>
    <p>Drag this link to your bookmarks bar, then click it on any page:</p>
    <p><a href={href}>deadhead</a></p>
    <p>
      It reads the rendered page, where frameworks have already assembled the head,
      and draws its findings in a panel. It loads nothing and runs no eval, so a
      strict Content-Security-Policy does not block it. Its full report button
      re-reads the page's HTML as the server sent it and opens it on{" "}
      <a href="/try">Try</a> for line numbers, advice and the fixed HTML. The HTML
      travels in the link fragment, which no server receives; where the page's CSP
      blocks the re-read, the button sends the rendered DOM. To run it from DevTools
      instead, paste <a href="/bookmarklet.js">bookmarklet.js</a> into Sources → Snippets.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/deadhead.css">deadhead.css</a> does the same without script: add it to
      a page and the offending tags outline themselves.
    </p>

    <h2>ESLint</h2>
    <p>
      <code>eslint-plugin-deadhead</code> reports the same findings through{" "}
      <code>@html-eslint/parser</code>, with autofix. It is a convenience for
      editor feedback: if your head tags live in JSX, Vue or Svelte, an HTML
      parser will not see them, and the bookmarklet will. It needs ESLint 10.
    </p>
    <pre><code>npm install --save-dev eslint eslint-plugin-deadhead</code></pre>
    <pre><code>// eslint.config.js
import &lbrace; defineConfig &rbrace; from "eslint/config";
import deadhead from "eslint-plugin-deadhead";

export default defineConfig([deadhead.configs.recommended]);</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>recommended</code> turns on every harmful and deprecated rule:
      harmful ones are errors and the rest warnings. <code>all</code> adds the
      unnecessary ones. Rule names are the rule ids:{" "}
      <code>deadhead/meta/keywords</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Full flag reference, configuration file and baseline documentation live in
      the{" "}
      <a href={REPO}>README</a>.
    </p>
</Layout>
